<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benjamin Mkapa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a>–<a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benjamin Mkapa was a Tanzanian journalist, diplomat and politician who served as the third president of Tanzania from 1995 to 2005, the first elected under the multi-party system. A protégé of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>, he pursued privatisation, fiscal discipline and debt relief, winning the support of international donors, and later mediated in regional conflicts, including in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benjamin-mkapa.jpg" alt="Benjamin Mkapa" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copyright World Economic Forum (www.weforum.org)/Photo by Zahur Ramji / Mediapix · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjamin_Mkapa_2010-05-07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Benjamin William Mkapa was born on 12 November 1938 in Lupaso, near Masasi, Tanganyika. He graduated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makerere-university">Makerere University</a> in Uganda in 1962 with a Bachelor of Arts in English. The following year he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a>, where he earned a master's degree in International Affairs.</p><p>He worked as an administrative officer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodoma">Dodoma</a> and served as Minister for Science, Technology and Higher Education. He headed the Tanzanian mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1982 and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1983 and 1984, and he was Minister of Foreign Affairs from 1977 to 1980 and again from 1984 to 1990.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1995 Mkapa was elected president on a popular anti-corruption campaign and with the strong support of the former president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>. He chaired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chama-cha-mapinduzi">Chama Cha Mapinduzi</a>, the Revolutionary State Political Party. His anti-corruption measures included an open forum, the Presidential Commission on Corruption, known as the Warioba Commission, and increased support for the Prevention of Corruption Bureau.</p><p>His second five-year term ended in December 2005. In it he privatised state-owned corporations and instituted free market policies, which his supporters argued would promote growth by attracting foreign investment. These policies won the support of the World Bank and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a> and led to the cancellation of some of Tanzania's foreign debts. He was criticised for the limited effect of his anti-corruption efforts and for lavish spending, including £15 million on a private presidential jet and almost £30 million on military aviation equipment from BAE Systems.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After he left office under the two-term limit, Mkapa faced accusations of corruption, among them that he and his former finance minister Daniel Yona had improperly taken over the Kiwira coal mine in the southern highlands. In 2007 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a>'s Panel of Eminent African Personalities, with Graça Machel and Kofi Annan as chair, which was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> to resolve the violence that followed disputed election results. Its mediation led to the National Accord and Reconciliation Act of 2008.</p><p>Mkapa was a trustee of the Aga Khan University from 2007 to 2012. He was treated for malaria on 22 July 2020 and died of a heart attack in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> in the early hours of 23 July, at the age of 81. He was laid to rest in his hometown of Lupaso. Kenya's president declared three days of national mourning in recognition of his role in resolving the violence of 2007 and 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benjamin Mkapa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Presidents have observed a limit of two five-year terms: <b>Benjamin Mkapa</b> (1995–2005), Jakaya Kikwete (2005–2015) and John Magufuli, who was elected in 2015, restricted the opposition and the press, and died in office in March 2021, early in his second term.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ndanda, in what is now <a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-10.5,39.02&amp;year=1991" aria-label="Ndanda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5625 2555 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Benjamin Mkapa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6075 2825h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6075 2825h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-10.5,39.02&amp;year=1991">What was here in 1991? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
